  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Sunday)\nIssued at 156 PM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n18Z surface analysis shows high pressure building in the northern \nGreat Plains behind yesterday's cold front. This combined with \nvisible satellite imagery shows clear skies today. Ongoing ridging \nover the western CONUS and Rocky Mountains is supporting light \nrising heights across the northern plains, keeping clouds minimal \nfor the next few days. Winds will be lighter than yesterday, but \nstill breezy with 15-20kt gusts expected across most of the area.\n\nMonday will be warm, with widespread high temperatures in the upper \n80s. West flow out of Wyoming will support a downsloping regime \non the eastern side of the Black Hills that may push temperatures \nup past 90. NBM 75th percentile shows most areas in western South \nDakota and south of I90 reaching 90 Monday afternoon. Deep mixing \nwill dry out relative humidities as well, keeping them around 20 \npercent in western South Dakota and the teens in far northeastern \nWyoming. Winds should remain between 10-15 mph for this same area \nso will forgo a Red Flag Warning at this point. A low pressure \nsystem currently spinning up in Canada will drape a weak frontal \nboundary through the area Monday night. This will cap temperatures\nin the mid 80s on Tuesday. \n\nLooking toward the rest of the week, temperatures stay seasonal in \nthe 70s and northwest upper flow will keep skies mostly clear. \nPattern change arrives next weekend as both GFS and Euro have a \nlarge trough crossing the Rockies. Guidance is showing a robust \nsystem impacting the northern plains the first half of next week. \nThis will be our next chance of meaningful precipitation and will \ncarry a transition to cooler weather.",
